const body = `<h1>2.9a — split(): One Line In, Many Pieces Out</h1>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Discovery Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        In 2.2c you built a command parser with <code>indexOf</code> and
        <code>substring</code>. It took a dozen lines to split "attack goblin" into two
        parts. Today you learn the method that does it in one. And it will carry the
        whole second half of this course: every data file you read, every save game you
        load, every line of the telemetry dataset is cut with <code>split</code>.
    </p>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Experiment 1: First Contact</h3>
        <p>Create <code>SplitLab.java</code>. Predict, then run:</p>
        <pre><code>String loot = "sword,iron,50";
String[] parts = loot.split(",");

System.out.println(parts.length);    // ?
System.out.println(parts[0]);        // ?
System.out.println(parts[1]);        // ?
System.out.println(parts[2]);        // ?</code></pre>
        <p>Four predictions, four confirmations. Then check the String table on the
        <a href="../exam/java-quick-reference.html">Java Quick Reference</a>: find
        <code>split</code> and read its one-line contract. It returns a
        <code>String[]</code>, an ARRAY of Strings. You have not formally met arrays
        yet (that is Unit 6), but here is all you need today: <code>parts[0]</code> is
        the first piece, <code>parts[1]</code> the second, and <code>parts.length</code>
        tells you how many pieces exist. That is enough to be dangerous.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Experiment 2: The Loot Table Loader</h3>
        <p>Here are three lines from a game's item database. Each one is a record:</p>
        <pre><code>iron sword,3,50,uncommon
health potion,1,25,common
flame tongue,8,400,rare</code></pre>
        <p>Write a program that loops over these three lines (a for-each over a
        String[] of the lines, or just three copies) and for each line prints:</p>
        <pre><code>NAME: iron sword | WEIGHT: 3 | VALUE: 50 gold | RARITY: uncommon</code></pre>
        <p>Rules: use <code>split(",")</code> once per line. The gold value must come
        out as an <code>int</code> in a variable named <code>value</code>. You will
        need the method you discovered on the JQR in 2.8a to turn "50" into 50.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Trap 1: pieces are Strings, not numbers.</strong>
        <code>split</code> gives you <code>"50"</code>, not <code>50</code>.
        <code>Integer.parseInt(parts[2])</code> makes the conversion. Arithmetic on
        un-converted pieces is a compile error, and the error message is your friend.<br><br>
        <strong>Trap 2: split does not trim.</strong> If the line has spaces after
        commas, like <code>"iron sword, 3, 50"</code>, then <code>parts[1]</code> is
        <code>" 3"</code> with a leading space, and <code>parseInt(" 3")</code> crashes.
        Test it. Then fix it. There is more than one fix; find the one you like.
    </div>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: The Empty Piece</h3>
        <pre><code>String line = "dragon,,500";
String[] p = line.split(",");
System.out.println(p.length);
System.out.println(p[1]);</code></pre>
        <p>Predict both. Run it. An empty field is still a piece: length 3, and
        <code>p[1]</code> is the empty String <code>""</code>. Now try
        <code>Integer.parseInt(p[1])</code> and read the error. This is THE data-file
        bug of the entire industry. Your monsters.txt and items.txt are written to be
        clean, but the moment a teammate edits a file by hand, empty fields happen.
        Guard your loader: check <code>parts.length</code> before indexing.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Experiment 3: One Split, Two Formats</h3>
        <p>The same item data also ships in a second format, space-separated:</p>
        <pre><code>potion 1 25 common
rope 1 10 common</code></pre>
        <p>Write <code>String[] q = line.split(" ");</code> and print the pieces.
        Now try <code>"scroll   of   might"</code> (multiple spaces). What happens to
        the empty pieces between spaces? Compare with splitting the comma format on
        commas. Write down: which delimiter is safer for data files, and why do you
        think the course data files use commas?</p>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Rewrite your 2.2c command parser using split. How many lines did it
            shrink from? Which version is easier to read? Which handles
            "attack goblin with sword" more naturally?</li>
            <li>Look at <a href="../data/monsters.txt">monsters.txt</a> (view it in the
            browser). Each line is a monster: name, hp, attack, gold, type. Without
            writing the loop yet, write the ONE line that turns one of those lines into
            pieces, and the one line that pulls out the hp as an int.</li>
            <li>The JQR lists <code>split</code> in the String table. Why would College
            Board add a method this powerful to the exam sheet in 2025? What can exams
            now test that they could not before? (Hint: you will do it in 6.6 and 7.6.)</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Split a line on a delimiter and index the pieces</li>
            <li>Convert numeric pieces to int/double with parseInt/parseDouble</li>
            <li>Predict the effect of empty fields and extra spaces, and guard against them</li>
            <li>Choose a delimiter for a data file and defend the choice</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/string-split.html">split() &amp; Data Lines</a> — full guide, delimiter choice, the empty-piece traps</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/string.html">The String Class</a> — split in the full method reference</li>
        <li><a href="../exam/java-quick-reference.html">Java Quick Reference</a> — the split row on your exam sheet</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
